{"meta":{"task":"dijkstra-universal","start_utc":"04:16","end_min":362.9,"model":"DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017","result":"proved"},"nodes":[{"id":"root","parent":null,"depth":0,"name":"dijkstra_universally_optimal (main theorem)","nl":"The main theorem: Dijkstra's algorithm with a working-set heap is universally optimal in time and in comparisons.","state":"accepted","how":"split s001","born":0.0,"done":362.7,"options":["x0001","x0002","x0003","x0004","x0005","x0006","x0031","x0032","x0033","x0044","x0045","x0046","x0105","x0106","x0128"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o001","parent":"root","depth":1,"name":"dijkstra_trace_exists","nl":"For every digraph with more than two vertices (all reachable from the source) and every non-negative arc-length assignment, there exists a Dijkstra trace: a scan order that is a true distance order together with one event (ins, relax or skip) for every outgoing arc of every scanned vertex, satisfying the resource and order invariants of the high-level algorithm.","state":"accepted","how":"direct proof","born":112.4,"done":146.4,"options":["x0010","x0021","x0022","x0026","x0027"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o002","parent":"root","depth":1,"name":"dijkstra_heap_slack","nl":"There is a universal constant C > 0 such that in every Dijkstra trace on every digraph with more than two vertices and every non-negative arc-length assignment, the heap cost of the working-set-bound heap (one unit per vertex plus log2 of the working-set size at each delete-min, summed over the vertices) is at most C\u00b7(m + log2 D) and also at most C\u00b7(F + log2 D), where m is the number of arcs, F is the maximum number ","state":"accepted","how":"split s005","born":112.4,"done":311.8,"options":["x0007","x0012","x0013","x0014","x0025","x0059","x0077"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o003","parent":"root","depth":1,"name":"dijkstra_comp_upper","nl":"There is a universal constant C > 0 such that every Dijkstra trace on every digraph with more than two vertices and every non-negative arc-length assignment makes at most C\u00b7(F + log2 D) comparisons, where F is the maximum number of forward arcs of a distance order and D is the number of distance orders.","state":"accepted","how":"direct proof","born":112.4,"done":322.2,"options":["x0011","x0028","x0029","x0030"],"deps":["o002"]},{"id":"o004","parent":"root","depth":1,"name":"dijkstra_time_lower","nl":"Given the interval-DAG bound, there is a universal constant C > 0 such that for every digraph with more than two vertices, every time-model algorithm that is correct on that vertex set and source has some non-negative arc-length assignment and legal representation on which it spends at least (m + log2 D)/C time.","state":"accepted","how":"split s004","born":112.4,"done":262.4,"options":["x0008","x0015","x0016","x0017","x0023","x0050"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o005","parent":"root","depth":1,"name":"dijkstra_comp_lower","nl":"Given the interval-DAG bound, there is a universal constant C > 0 such that for every digraph with more than two vertices, every correct comparison-model decision tree for that graph has some non-negative arc-length assignment on which it makes at least (F - n + 1 + log2 D)/C comparisons.","state":"accepted","how":"split s003","born":112.4,"done":359.3,"options":["x0009","x0018","x0019","x0020","x0024","x0034"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o006","parent":"o001","depth":2,"name":"exists_dist_rank","nl":"There is a rank function ell on the vertices such that every vertex other than the source has an incoming arc (u, v) which is exact for the true distance, d(v) = d(u) + w(u,v), and along which the rank strictly decreases, ell(u) < ell(v).","state":"superseded","how":"","born":null,"done":null,"options":[],"deps":[]},{"id":"o007","parent":"o001","depth":2,"name":"true_dist_order_of_rank","nl":"Given a vertex rank with the shortest-path predecessor property, the vertex order which sorts the vertices by the lexicographic key (true distance, rank, vertex) is a true distance order: it is non-decreasing in true distance and every non-source vertex has an incoming arc from an earlier vertex.","state":"superseded","how":"","born":null,"done":null,"options":[],"deps":[]},{"id":"o008","parent":"o001","depth":2,"name":"trace_of_true_dist_order","nl":"Given a true distance order L, there is a Dijkstra trace whose scan order is L: scan the vertices in L-order, and in the block of each scanned vertex emit one event per outgoing arc, inserting each vertex in the block of its earliest in-neighbour, skipping arcs into already scanned vertices and relaxing the others.","state":"superseded","how":"","born":null,"done":null,"options":[],"deps":[]},{"id":"o009","parent":"o005","depth":2,"name":"comp_lower_unique_weights","nl":"For every digraph and every distance order L of it, there is a non-negative arc-length assignment for which L is a true distance order, and moreover the only true distance order: the true distances are strictly increasing along L, so the order of the vertices by distance is unique and equals L. (Two constructions: the powers-of-3 weights 3^(L v) on forward arcs and 3^(n+1) on backward arcs, or the canonical weights c","state":"accepted","how":"direct proof","born":165.2,"done":156.8,"options":["x0036","x0041"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o010","parent":"o005","depth":2,"name":"comp_lower_ternary_leaves","nl":"Let T be a comparison tree on a digraph G with more than two vertices. If every distance order of G occurs as the output of T on some non-negative arc-length assignment, then some non-negative arc-length assignment makes T perform at least log_3(D) comparisons, where D is the number of distance orders; equivalently log_2(D) <= log_2(3) times the number of comparisons. This is the ternary leaf count: distinct outputs ","state":"accepted","how":"direct proof","born":165.2,"done":161.0,"options":["x0037","x0042","x0043"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o011","parent":"o005","depth":2,"name":"comp_lower_excess_arcs","nl":"Given the interval-DAG bound, there is a universal constant C > 0 such that for every digraph with more than two vertices and every correct comparison-model decision tree T for it, there is a non-negative arc-length assignment on which T makes at least (F - n + 1)/C comparisons, where F is the maximum number of forward arcs of a distance order and n is the number of vertices. This is the excess-forward-arc adversary:","state":"accepted","how":"split s006","born":165.2,"done":356.1,"options":["x0035","x0038","x0039","x0040","x0047","x0048","x0049","x0053","x0054","x0075","x0076","x0080","x0098","x0099","x0100","x0101","x0102","x0103"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o012","parent":"o004","depth":2,"name":"time_lower_tree_access","nl":"For every distance order L of G and every non-negative weight w satisfying 3^(L v) <= d(v) < (3/2) 3^(L v) for v != src, every correct time-tree algorithm A spends at least (n - 1)/8 time on (w, rep) for every legal representation rep: the n-1 parent arcs of L are all read.","state":"superseded","how":"","born":200.2,"done":null,"options":["x0052","x0056"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o013","parent":"o004","depth":2,"name":"time_lower_excess_access","nl":"Given a family f of canonical unique-distance-order weights as in Lemma 6.1, every correct time-tree algorithm A on G spends at least (m - n + 1)/8 time on some input: the m - n + 1 excess arcs cannot all be avoided.","state":"superseded","how":"","born":200.2,"done":null,"options":["x0051","x0055","x0057","x0058","x0065"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o014","parent":"o002","depth":2,"name":"heap_ins_parent","nl":"Every Dijkstra trace has an insertion parent map: each non-source vertex is inserted in exactly one block, that block's vertex is a scanned in-neighbour scanned earlier, and the vertex is the parent of all its insert events; the source is its own parent.","state":"accepted","how":"direct proof","born":221.9,"done":236.0,"options":["x0061"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o015","parent":"o002","depth":2,"name":"heap_window","nl":"For the insertion parent map of a trace: the working-set size is positive, the cumulative insertion count starts at 1 and grows by the number of children of the scanned vertex, scan positions and insertion ranks are injective and bounded, parents are scanned before children, and every insertion rank lies in the window of its parent's block.","state":"accepted","how":"direct proof","born":221.9,"done":279.0,"options":["x0062","x0067","x0071","x0072","x0073"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o016","parent":"o002","depth":2,"name":"forest_hook","nl":"Hook-length formula for a rooted tree given by a parent map and a linear extension position: the number of linear extensions times the product of the subtree sizes equals n factorial.","state":"superseded","how":"","born":221.9,"done":null,"options":["x0064","x0069","x0074","x0078","x0079","x0092","x0093","x0094"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o017","parent":"o002","depth":2,"name":"heap_product","nl":"For abstract parent, position, insertion-rank and cumulative-count data satisfying the tree, injectivity, monotonicity and window hypotheses, and assuming the hook-length identity for all rooted trees, the product over all vertices of A (pos v) - ins v is at most 16 to the n times the number of linear extensions of the tree.","state":"accepted","how":"direct proof","born":221.9,"done":300.4,"options":["x0060","x0063","x0066","x0068","x0070"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o018","parent":"o011","depth":3,"name":"correct_tree_cell","nl":"Let T be a correct comparison tree on a digraph G and L a vertex order whose true distances at some non-negative weight w\u2080 are strictly increasing along L. Then there are a number d, d linear functionals \u2113s on the arc lengths, and a strictly positive weight w\u2081 such that: w\u2081 strictly satisfies the d inequalities (and d is at most the number of comparisons T makes at w\u2081), and every non-negative weight satisfying the in","state":"accepted","how":"split s007","born":293.2,"done":352.7,"options":["x0081","x0084","x0086","x0087","x0088","x0107"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o019","parent":"o01