th":4,"name":"strict_order_perturb","nl":"If a non-negative weight w\u2080 has true distances strictly increasing along L, then there is a strictly positive weight w\u2081 with the same property, and a radius \u03b4 > 0 such that every weight within \u03b4 of w\u2081 (coordinatewise) still has true distances strictly increasing along L.","state":"superseded","how":"","born":340.7,"done":null,"options":["x0109","x0115","x0116","x0118","x0120"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o023","parent":"o018","depth":4,"name":"eval_eq_of_strict","nl":"A correct comparison tree outputs L at every non-negative weight whose true distances are strictly increasing along L (the strictly increasing order is the unique distance order of such a weight).","state":"accepted","how":"direct proof","born":340.7,"done":352.5,"options":["x0110","x0119","x0121","x0122","x0124"],"deps":[]},{"id":"o024","parent":"o018","depth":4,"name":"cell_containment","nl":"If a correct tree evaluates to L on the ray w + \u03b5\u00b7w\u2081 for every non-negative cell point w with \u2113s_i(w) \u2265 0 and every \u03b5 > 0, then on the whole closed cell the true distances are non-decreasing along L.","state":"superseded","how":"","born":340.7,"done":null,"options":["x0111","x0123","x0125","x0126","x0127"],"deps":[]}],"splits":[{"id":"s001","parent":"root","author":"x0002","audit":"x0006","children":["o001","o002","o003","o004","o005"],"reused":[],"status":"accepted","at":112.4},{"id":"s002","parent":"o001","author":"x0026","audit":null,"children":["o006","o007","o008"],"reused":[],"status":"superseded","at":null},{"id":"s003","parent":"o005","author":"x0024","audit":"x0034","children":["o009","o010","o011"],"reused":[],"status":"accepted","at":165.2},{"id":"s004","parent":"o004","author":"x0023","audit":"x0050","children":["o012","o013"],"reused":[],"status":"accepted","at":200.2},{"id":"s005","parent":"o002","author":"x0025","audit":"x0059","children":["o014","o015","o016","o017"],"reused":[],"status":"accepted","at":221.9},